{"name":"Extra Mile","hex":"#91916d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"extra mile","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make an extra effort; to do a particularly good job."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/extra_mile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Extra Mile (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Extra Mile is the third studio album by the American country music band Shenandoah. It was released May 2, 1990 on Columbia Records. Their final album for the label, it produced five chart singles on the Billboard country charts. In order of release, these were \"Next to You, Next to Me\", \"Ghost in This House\", \"I Got You\", \"The Moon Over Georgia\" and \"When You Were Mine\". \"Next to You, Next to Me\" was the band's fourth Number One, and all the others except \"When You Were Mine\" were Top Ten hits. Extra Mile is certified gold by the RIAA."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/49/Extramile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_Mile_(album)"},{"title":"Al Denson","blocks":[{"text":"Albert Wray Denson, more commonly known as Al Denson, is a contemporary Christian music artist and a Christian radio and television show host."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al_Denson"},{"title":"Mile","blocks":[{"text":"The mile, sometimes the international mile or statute mile to distinguish it from other miles, is a British imperial unit and United States customary unit of length; both are based on the older English unit of length equal to 5,280 English feet, or 1,760 yards. The statute mile was standardised as a unit of length between the Commonwealth of Nations and the United States by an international agreement in 1959, when the yard was formally redefined with respect to SI units as 0.9144 metres, making the mile exactly 1609.344 metres. For everyday use, five miles equates roughly to eight kilometres. Mileage is the term used informally for a distance expressed in miles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Milestone%2C_Knightsbridge%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1590514.jpg/330px-Milestone%2C_Knightsbridge%2C_London_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1590514.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mile"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/500px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/1280px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG/500px-RomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The supposed remains of the Golden Milestone, the zero-mile marker of the Roman road network, in the Roman Forum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARomaForoRomanoMiliariumAureum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/500px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/1280px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Mercator_scale.png/500px-Mercator_scale.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scalebar on a 16th-century map made by Mercator. The scalebar is expressed in \"Hours walking or common Flemish miles\", and includes three actual scales: small, medium and big Flemish miles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMercator_scale.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Wegmasse1.png/500px-Wegmasse1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Wegmasse1.png/500px-Wegmasse1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various historic miles and leagues from an 1848 German textbook, given in feet, metres, and fractions of a \"degree of meridian\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWegmasse1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg/500px-Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg/500px-Milestone%2C_Mountbellow_%28geograph_5365674%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Milestone on Mountbellew Bridge, erected c. 1760. Distances are given in Irish miles.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMilestone%2C_Mountbellow_(geograph_5365674).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG/500px-Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG/1280px-Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG/500px-Edinburgh_High_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Edinburgh's \"Royal Mile\"—running from the castle to Holyrood Abbey—is roughly a Scots mile long.[53]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEdinburgh_High